\section*{Angular-factor dichotomy for triangular billiards}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Manuscript 1 (family 150, 2026-09-25): The full unit-speed billiard flow of each nondegenerate triangle with an angle irrational relative to $\pi$ is claimed to be ergodic, without a Diophantine or genericity condition. Manuscript 2 (family 150, 2026-10-05): An irrational angle divided by $\pi$ is claimed to force weak mixing for the full billiard flow of any nondegenerate Euclidean triangle, using normalized Liouville measure.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Let $Q$ be the interior of any Euclidean triangle that is nondegenerate and has an angle whose ratio to $\pi$ is irrational. For the unit-speed specular billiard flow $\Phi_t$ on $Q\times S^1$, discarding trajectories that hit vertices in either time direction, OpenAI claims ergodicity with respect to
\[
d\mu=\frac{dA\,d\theta}{2\pi\operatorname{Area}(Q)}.
\]
Precisely, if a measurable $A$ satisfies $\mu(\Phi_t^{-1}A\mathbin{\triangle} A)=0$ for every real $t$, then $\mu(A)$ is $0$ or $1$. This ranges over every such triangle, rather than almost every parameter or a fixed direction.

\paragraph{Mathematical claim 2.} For every nondegenerate Euclidean triangle $Q$ having at least one angle irrational relative to $\pi$, OpenAI claims that its unit-speed specular billiard flow $\Phi_t$ is weakly mixing under normalized Liouville measure
\[
d\mu=\frac{dA\,d\theta}{2\pi\operatorname{Area}(Q)}.
\]
Equivalently, the simultaneous product flow $(x,y)\mapsto(\Phi_tx,\Phi_ty)$ is ergodic for $\mu\otimes\mu$. Vertex-hitting trajectories are discarded. Weak mixing is the claim, without an assertion of ordinary mixing.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 150, 2026-09-25): On the flat double, positional smoothing and long cone-avoiding segments bound endpoint gradients of angular Fourier tails. Two successive weak-limit passages show that all coefficient gradients vanish without a Sobolev assumption; irrational cone rotation removes every nonzero angular mode, leaving a constant invariant function.

Manuscript 2 (family 150, 2026-10-05): Local transport estimates force a possible eigenfunction to be a plane wave with only direction-dependent amplitude. Reflections through the irrational vertex make that amplitude constant, and reflection at the third side excludes any nonzero frequency; the spectral criterion then gives weak mixing.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 150, 2026-09-25): The problem definition assumes all three angle/pi ratios are irrational. The theorem needs only one irrational angle and proves ergodicity (October proves the stronger weak mixing property).

Manuscript 2 (family 150, 2026-10-05): The problem definition assumes all three angle/pi ratios are irrational. The theorem needs only one irrational angle and proves ergodicity (October proves the stronger weak mixing property).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 150, the scope page documents the following selections. The selection establishes ergodicity and the almost-everywhere flow law for an irrational-angle triangle, including the exceptional-trajectory null set. The weak-mixing companion is not selected. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Manuscript 2 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: IrrationalTriangleBilliard.lean. Declarations: OAI.TriangularBilliards.irrational\_triangle\_billiard.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

